@page "/"
@inject KsaModsApi Api

<PageTitle>ksamods.gg | Mods for Kitten Space Agency</PageTitle>

@*
    No hero graphic. This is a catalogue, and the useful things are the search field and the list.

    The headline says what the site is before it says what it does differently. It used to open
    with "Find mods that actually load", which is the interesting claim and a poor introduction:
    somebody arriving cold cannot tell from it whether this is a mod index, a compatibility
    checker or a launcher. The differentiator earns its place in the sentence underneath, once
    the reader knows what they are looking at.
*@
<section class="max-w-2xl pb-10">
    <p class="eyebrow text-primary">Kitten Space Agency</p>

    <h1 class="mt-4 text-balance text-[2.5rem] font-bold leading-[1.05] tracking-tight sm:text-[3.25rem]">
        Find mods for KSA.
    </h1>

    <p class="mt-5 max-w-lg text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
        A catalogue of what people have made, with every release unpacked and read the same way
        the game reads it. If an archive is broken, you find out here rather than after you have
        installed it.
    </p>

    <form action="/mods" method="get" class="mt-7 flex gap-2">
        <input name="q" type="search" placeholder="Search mods…" aria-label="Search mods"
               class="h-10 flex-1 rounded-md border border-input bg-card px-3.5 text-sm
                      placeholder:text-muted-foreground focus-visible:outline-2
                      focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-ring" />
        <Button Type="submit" Class="h-10 px-5">Search</Button>
    </form>

    @* Answers the question the header's Sign in button raises for everyone who does not need it. *@
    <p class="mt-3 text-xs text-muted-foreground">
        Browsing and downloading need no account.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="border-t border-border pt-10">
    <div class="mb-5 flex items-baseline justify-between gap-4">
        <h2 class="eyebrow text-muted-foreground">Recently updated</h2>
        <a href="/mods" class="inline-flex min-h-6 items-center text-[0.8125rem] text-muted-foreground transition-colors hover:text-foreground">
            Browse all
        </a>
    </div>

    @if (_error is not null)
    {
        <Alert Variant="error" Title="The catalogue is unavailable">@_error</Alert>
    }
    else if (_mods is null)
    {
        <p class="font-mono text-xs text-muted-foreground">Loading…</p>
    }
    else if (_mods.Count == 0)
    {
        <Card>
            <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
                Nothing here yet. If you've made a mod, it takes one form and a connected
                repository to get it listed.
            </p>
            <Button Href="/mods/new" Class="mt-4" Size="sm">Add a mod</Button>
        </Card>
    }
    else
    {
        <div class="grid items-start gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
            @foreach (var mod in _mods)
            {
                <ModCard Mod="mod" />
            }
        </div>
    }
</section>

@*
    Loaders get a line rather than cards. There are one or two of them in the whole ecosystem and
    nobody browses for one - you install it because a mod said it needed it - so a card in the grid
    above would take a slot from the content this page exists to show, every time one was updated.
*@
@if (_loaders is { Count: > 0 })
{
    <section class="mt-8 rounded-lg border border-border bg-card/40 px-5 py-4">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-3 gap-y-1">
            <h2 class="eyebrow text-muted-foreground">Loaders</h2>
            <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
                The code mods run on. Install one when a mod asks for it.
            </p>
            <a href="/mods?type=mod-loader"
               class="ml-auto text-[0.8125rem] text-muted-foreground transition-colors hover:text-foreground">
                All loaders
            </a>
        </div>

        <ul class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap gap-x-5 gap-y-2">
            @foreach (var loader in _loaders)
            {
                <li>
                    <a href="@($"/mods/{loader.Id}")"
                       class="text-sm font-medium underline-offset-4 hover:underline">@loader.Name</a>
                    <code class="ml-1.5 font-mono text-xs text-muted-foreground">@loader.Id</code>
                </li>
            }
        </ul>
    </section>
}

@*
    Two audiences with almost nothing in common: one wants to install things, the other wants to
    publish them. Writing for both at once produced copy that told players about repositories and
    authors about compatibility badges. Splitting them is also the honest place to answer "why
    would I sign up" - the answer is that most people never need to, and the ones who do are
    signing in to prove a listing is theirs.
*@
<section class="mt-14 border-t border-border pt-10">
    <div class="grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
        <Card Header="@(b => b.AddContent(0, "Playing"))">
            <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
                Search the catalogue, tell us which game build you are on, and see what fits it.
                Every listing says where the download really comes from and what the archive
                contains - including which mods lay claim to the same parts, which is the usual
                reason two of them will not run together.
            </p>

            <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
                <Button Href="/mods" Size="sm">Browse mods</Button>
                <Button Href="/collisions" Variant="outline" Size="sm">Check asset ids</Button>
            </div>

            <p class="mt-3 text-xs text-muted-foreground">
                None of this needs an account, and there is nothing to install to use it.
            </p>
        </Card>

        <Card Header="@(b => b.AddContent(0, "Making mods"))">
            <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
                Keep publishing exactly as you do now. Connect the repository you release from and
                every tag you push turns into a listing on its own - unpacked, checked, and
                pointing at your own download. We never host the file or stand between you and the
                people installing it.
            </p>

            <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
                <Button Href="/mods/new" Size="sm">List a mod</Button>
                <Button Href="/api-reference" Variant="outline" Size="sm">Read the API</Button>
            </div>

            <p class="mt-3 text-xs text-muted-foreground">
                This is the part that needs an account: connecting the repository is how we know
                the listing is yours.
            </p>
        </Card>
    </div>
</section>

@code {
    private IReadOnlyList<ModSummary>? _mods;
    private IReadOnlyList<ModSummary>? _loaders;
    private string? _error;

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        try
        {
            // Two requests rather than one filtered in the browser, so the six cards are six mods
            // whether or not a loader happens to have been updated this week.
            var page = await Api.SearchAsync(limit: 6, type: "mod");
            _mods = page?.Items ?? [];

            var loaders = await Api.SearchAsync(limit: 6, type: "mod-loader");
            _loaders = loaders?.Items ?? [];
        }
        catch (Exception ex) when (ex is ApiException or HttpRequestException or TaskCanceledException)
        {
            // The API being down should degrade this page, not blank it: the header, the search
            // box and the explanation are all still useful.
            _error = "The API isn't responding. This page will fill in once it's back.";
        }
    }
}
